  "plot_summary": "Iron Man is chairing the Avengers monthly meeting, insisting that they <i>must</i> find the Hulk.&nbsp; He uses an image caster to send a ghostly image of himself out to the FF, Spider-Man and the X-Men, but none of them have seen the Hulk and seem too busy to help.&nbsp; Thor suggests that perhaps Rick Jones might be of help since he knows the Hulk personally.\n\nOut west, Rick receives their ham radio request and begins hunting through a desert (complete with cactus - what is he <i>thinking</i>?).&nbsp; By sheer luck (<i>or plot laziness</i>), he encounters the Hulk, out near their old cave, and he convinces him to turn himself back to Bruce Banner using the cannon ray machine.&nbsp; But the dose apparently wasn't strong enough, and while sleeping, he again becomes the Hulk and smashes his way out.&nbsp; Rick runs to the Teen Brigade to call the Avengers to inform them he's on a rampage in New Mexico!\n\nTony Stark receives the message, becomes Iron Man and summons the rest of the Avengers, which includes a one-panel laugh-inducing scene of Don Blake telling Jane he's closing the office early, to which she thinks \"Oh, Don, if only you didn't <i>pamper</i> yourself so!&nbsp; If only you were more <i>rugged</i>!\"&nbsp; (<i>Heh, wow</i>!)\n\nIron Man makes it to New Mexico first and is immediately attacked by the Hulk.&nbsp; Giant-Man, Wasp and Iron Man all try their hand at stopping him before Thor finally arrives.&nbsp; Hulk jumps onto a train and actually hurls a car at them!&nbsp; Hulk tosses flour into the smoke stack, providing a smoke screen that allows him to slip away unseen.&nbsp; The text reads \"throughout the countryside, the search goes on.\"&nbsp; He sneaks aboard a truck and is dumped into a stream, which leads out to the \"cold Atlantic\" - believe it or not - \"after days in the water.\"&nbsp; (<i>Huh?</i>  How does he end up in the Atlantic from New Mexico?&nbsp; The Gulf of Mexico or Gulf of California, perhaps - but the <i>Atlantic</i>?&nbsp; Not buying it!&nbsp; The search through the countryside implied to me that it was still the countryside of New Mexico - there was no hint that he'd headed all the way to the east coast.)&nbsp; He's finally retrieved from the water by a ship where he spends some time recovering.\n\nBut we see the Sub-Mariner monitoring his every move.&nbsp; He meets the Hulk on a small deserted island, where the Hulk goes to \"plan my next moves against the human race that hates me!\"&nbsp; (<i>Gee, the Hulk is sure a sourpuss</i>!)&nbsp; The Hulk mistakes Sub-Mariner for a \"puny human\" and attempts to hurl him away, but the Sub-Mariner can fight back, and once in the water, he proves he's a match for the Hulk, and the two eventually learn they share a \"burning hatred for the human race\" and should team up.&nbsp; \"Together, we can bring humanity to its knees!\"&nbsp; The Hulk consents.&nbsp; Naturally, they both think that once the Avengers are beaten, they'll destroy each other.&nbsp; Sub-Mariner has a \"command ship\" that he uses to take them to Gibraltar, where he contacts the Avengers to let them know that he and the Hulk challenge them to battle!\n\nSoon, the Avengers head to Gibraltar in Tony Stark's experimental deep-sea jet bathyscaph.&nbsp; The text says that \"three of the world's most colorful evil-smashers arrive\" - <i>what</i>?&nbsp; Three?&nbsp; (I'm assuming Stan Lee omitted the Wasp in that count.&nbsp; What is <i>she</i> then?&nbsp; Chopped liver?&nbsp; Window dressing?)\n\nUsing some old abandoned WWII weapons, the Hulk and Sub-Mariner strike!&nbsp; Iron Man is hit by emery dust which stiffens his joints, and Giant-Man and the Wasp are forced to use oxygen to blow the dust out so he can move again while Thor takes on the evil pair.&nbsp; They all eventually re-engage, and both the Hulk and Sub-Mariner attempt to take Thor's hammer out of his grasp, but there is no real chance of that.\n\nSuddenly, the Hulk abruptly changes back to \"Bob\" Banner (<i>hey, read the text if you don't believe me</i>) and flees.&nbsp; \"Perhaps it's better this way!&nbsp; As Bob Banner, I've no hatred for the Avengers.\"&nbsp; As the Hulk has now left the scene, the Avengers begin to overwhelm the Sub-Mariner, who has now been out of the water long enough where he begins to weaken.&nbsp; Thor leaves to go search for the Hulk, and Iron Man tries to pin Subby to the wall, but it cracks it behind him, letting water into the chamber, allowing Namor to be re-invigorated.\n\nHe leaves, and Thor says \"I have too much respect for his valor!&nbsp; Namor has <i>earned</i> his escape!\"&nbsp; While it's a shame he isn't on their side, Iron Man declares they've made a bad mistake - \"we may live to regret this!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "There was nothing really <i>wrong</i> with the book, aside from a couple of impossibilities of ending up in the Atlantic and the randomness of going to Gibraltar, which seemed to come from nowhere.&nbsp; Having the Hulk team up with the Sub-Mariner makes a good bit of sense, given their mutual distrust of humans, so that was a positive element of this story.&nbsp; It was exactly the sort of menace that the Avengers were formed to take on, so that was all good.&nbsp; And I'm even okay with the Sub-Mariner's decision to go after the <i>Avengers</i> rather than the FF, given that he was trying to accommodate his new partner, who couldn't care less about the FF.\n\nBut there was also nothing really <i>engaging</i> about the story either.&nbsp; The Avengers battled the Hulk first, page after page and eventually battled the pair of them after the Hulk mysteriously was able to navigate from New Mexico to the <i>Atlantic Ocean</i>, completely by water - <i>what</i>?.&nbsp; As with many of the Lee/Kirby battle issues, the action gets a bit tiresome and rarely is resolved when heroes battle heroes.&nbsp; The Hulk's transformation back to \"Bob\" Banner was <i>completely</i> random and seemed to have more to do with them running out of pages than anything else, which soured the ending.&nbsp; Also, \"Bob\" Banner was correctly identified earlier in the issue as \"Dr. <i>Bruce</i> Banner\" - how on earth did such a blunder make it out of the editing phase?\n\nHowever, on the whole, it was a solid issue, and I award it a slightly above mid-grade rating - the ending definitely prevented it from grading higher.\n\nJack Kirby's checkerboard rendition of Spider-Man's mask was a bit stark and looked markedly different than what he drew just the previous month in [LINK:1963:ASM_8|ASM #8|2027-02-12] (though he admittedly had Steve Ditko's help inking to correct any such obvious mistakes).&nbsp; Also, once again, the full-size Jan sometimes is drawn with wings and sometimes without - very inconsistent, where her wings are really only supposed to show when shrunk to wasp-size.\n\nIt seems Rick Jones is back in New Mexico, after having been randomly placed in NYC in issue 2.&nbsp; I can't really get my head around this (and apparently neither can Lee/Kirby).\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME - write something</b>\n\n\n\nAdditional Note:&nbsp; about a year later, we find out there's more to this story, revealed by Thor in the pages of <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> #112!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}